1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,834
In many London Underground stations,

2
00:00:02,834 --> 00:00:05,590
if you look down from the platform,

3
00:00:05,590 --> 00:00:08,267
the space between the rails is not

4
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:08,661
flat.

5
00:00:08,841 --> 00:00:10,630
There is a deep trench,

6
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:12,186
almost a meter deep,

7
00:00:12,186 --> 00:00:14,053
running under the track.

8
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:16,509
It is there for drainage and for

9
00:00:16,509 --> 00:00:17,078
workers.

10
00:00:17,258 --> 00:00:18,817
But it has another,

11
00:00:18,817 --> 00:00:19,719
darker job.

12
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:22,324
It has saved the lives of people

13
00:00:22,324 --> 00:00:23,840
who fell, or jumped,

14
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,356
in front of a train.

15
00:00:25,536 --> 00:00:28,554
Today, eleven parts of a subway,

16
00:00:28,554 --> 00:00:30,818
and why each one exists.

17
00:00:31,168 --> 00:00:35,434
The London Underground opened in eighteen sixty-three,

18
00:00:35,434 --> 00:00:38,831
the first underground railway in the world.

19
00:00:39,011 --> 00:00:41,541
Since then, cities from New York to

20
00:00:41,541 --> 00:00:43,493
Tokyo have built their own.

21
00:00:43,673 --> 00:00:46,471
A subway is a strange machine.

22
00:00:46,651 --> 00:00:48,315
Millions of people,

23
00:00:48,315 --> 00:00:49,979
thousands of volts,

24
00:00:49,979 --> 00:00:53,220
and trains running every two minutes,

25
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:55,497
all inside narrow tunnels.

26
00:00:55,677 --> 00:00:58,127
Almost every detail you see in a

27
00:00:58,127 --> 00:01:02,260
station exists because something went wrong somewhere.

28
00:01:02,610 --> 00:01:05,503
As always, every fact comes from a

29
00:01:05,503 --> 00:01:08,566
named source, transport authorities,

30
00:01:08,566 --> 00:01:11,714
medical studies, and railway history,

31
00:01:11,714 --> 00:01:14,267
all listed in the description.

32
00:01:14,447 --> 00:01:15,852
Let's start with the pit.

33
00:01:16,201 --> 00:01:18,505
Why is there a deep pit between

34
00:01:18,505 --> 00:01:21,031
the rails at many subway stations?

35
00:01:21,211 --> 00:01:24,973
Officially, it is mostly a drainage pit.

36
00:01:25,153 --> 00:01:27,076
Water collects in tunnels,

37
00:01:27,076 --> 00:01:29,147
and the pit carries it away.

38
00:01:29,327 --> 00:01:33,075
It also gives maintenance workers access to

39
00:01:33,075 --> 00:01:35,167
the underside of trains.

40
00:01:35,347 --> 00:01:37,853
But it has a famous nickname in

41
00:01:37,853 --> 00:01:39,793
London, the suicide pit.

42
00:01:39,973 --> 00:01:42,483
If a person falls or jumps onto

43
00:01:42,483 --> 00:01:45,640
the track, they can sometimes drop into

44
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:48,392
the pit, and the train passes over

45
00:01:48,392 --> 00:01:48,797
them.

46
00:01:49,146 --> 00:01:50,157
Does it work?

47
00:01:50,337 --> 00:01:53,025
A study published in the British Medical

48
00:01:53,025 --> 00:01:56,251
Journal in nineteen ninety-nine looked at people

49
00:01:56,251 --> 00:01:58,940
hit by trains on the London Underground.

50
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:00,937
At stations with pits,

51
00:02:00,937 --> 00:02:03,415
about forty-four percent died.

52
00:02:03,595 --> 00:02:05,858
At stations without pits,

53
00:02:05,858 --> 00:02:08,483
about sixty-six percent died.

54
00:02:08,663 --> 00:02:11,391
The pit does not prevent the accident.

55
00:02:11,571 --> 00:02:13,670
But it gives a person a place

56
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:14,683
to survive it.

57
00:02:15,032 --> 00:02:17,730
Many of these pits were built in

58
00:02:17,730 --> 00:02:20,090
the early twentieth century,

59
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:22,788
as the deep tube lines expanded.

60
00:02:22,968 --> 00:02:25,830
Today, the best protection is to stop

61
00:02:25,830 --> 00:02:28,770
people from reaching the track at all,

62
00:02:28,770 --> 00:02:31,246
with doors on the platform edge.

63
00:02:31,426 --> 00:02:34,185
But on lines built a hundred years

64
00:02:34,185 --> 00:02:36,458
ago, the pit is still there,

65
00:02:36,458 --> 00:02:39,786
quietly doing a job most passengers never

66
00:02:39,786 --> 00:02:40,354
notice.

67
00:02:40,703 --> 00:02:42,383
Why does one rail in the New

68
00:02:42,383 --> 00:02:44,483
York subway have a little roof over

69
00:02:44,483 --> 00:02:44,662
it?

70
00:02:44,842 --> 00:02:46,565
That is the third rail,

71
00:02:46,565 --> 00:02:49,711
and it carries the electricity that powers

72
00:02:49,711 --> 00:02:52,482
the trains, around six hundred volts.

73
00:02:52,662 --> 00:02:55,485
Trains collect the power with a metal

74
00:02:55,485 --> 00:02:57,468
shoe that slides along it.

75
00:02:57,648 --> 00:03:00,730
The cover board on top protects people

76
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:04,055
and workers from touching it by accident,

77
00:03:04,055 --> 00:03:06,488
and it also keeps snow and ice

78
00:03:06,488 --> 00:03:08,434
off in outdoor sections.

79
00:03:08,783 --> 00:03:11,140
Touching the third rail can kill.

80
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:14,081
That is why subway workers are trained

81
00:03:14,081 --> 00:03:15,534
to never step on it,

82
00:03:15,534 --> 00:03:17,713
and why the rail is mounted on

83
00:03:17,713 --> 00:03:20,474
insulators slightly to the side of the

84
00:03:20,474 --> 00:03:21,491
running rails.

85
00:03:21,671 --> 00:03:24,137
Six hundred volts does not sound like

86
00:03:24,137 --> 00:03:27,403
much compared with the twenty-five thousand volts

87
00:03:27,403 --> 00:03:28,936
of some overhead wires,

88
00:03:28,936 --> 00:03:31,469
but the current available is enormous.

89
00:03:31,649 --> 00:03:34,448
Every year, people are killed on third

90
00:03:34,448 --> 00:03:36,143
rails around the world.

91
00:03:36,492 --> 00:03:38,877
Why not use overhead wires,

92
00:03:38,877 --> 00:03:40,997
like most modern trains?

93
00:03:41,177 --> 00:03:43,685
In tunnels, there is very little space

94
00:03:43,685 --> 00:03:44,741
above the train.

95
00:03:44,921 --> 00:03:47,528
A third rail lets tunnels be smaller

96
00:03:47,528 --> 00:03:48,904
and cheaper to dig.

97
00:03:49,084 --> 00:03:52,105
Many subways built in the late eighteen

98
00:03:52,105 --> 00:03:55,669
hundreds and early nineteen hundreds chose the

99
00:03:55,669 --> 00:03:58,148
third rail, and once a system is

100
00:03:58,148 --> 00:04:00,705
built, it is very hard to change.

101
00:04:01,055 --> 00:04:03,889
Why does the London Underground have four

102
00:04:03,889 --> 00:04:05,479
rails instead of three?

103
00:04:05,659 --> 00:04:07,497
On most electric railways,

104
00:04:07,497 --> 00:04:10,042
the current comes in through a power

105
00:04:10,042 --> 00:04:13,153
rail, and returns through the running rails,

106
00:04:13,153 --> 00:04:15,133
the ones the wheels roll on.

107
00:04:15,313 --> 00:04:18,367
London's tube lines have a separate return

108
00:04:18,367 --> 00:04:19,749
rail in the middle,

109
00:04:19,749 --> 00:04:21,640
between the running rails.

110
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:24,315
The power rail on the outside is

111
00:04:24,315 --> 00:04:27,590
positive, and the center rail is negative.

112
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:30,901
One important reason is the tunnels.

113
00:04:31,081 --> 00:04:34,137
Many old London tunnels are lined with

114
00:04:34,137 --> 00:04:35,423
cast iron rings.

115
00:04:35,603 --> 00:04:38,335
If the return current flowed through the

116
00:04:38,335 --> 00:04:40,794
running rails, some of it could leak

117
00:04:40,794 --> 00:04:43,184
into the ground and into those iron

118
00:04:43,184 --> 00:04:44,414
linings and pipes,

119
00:04:44,414 --> 00:04:47,145
and slowly eat them away by electrolytic

120
00:04:47,145 --> 00:04:47,828
corrosion.

121
00:04:48,008 --> 00:04:51,167
A separate insulated return rail keeps the

122
00:04:51,167 --> 00:04:53,047
current where it belongs.

123
00:04:53,397 --> 00:04:56,606
The fourth rail also makes signaling easier,

124
00:04:56,606 --> 00:04:59,305
because the running rails are free to

125
00:04:59,305 --> 00:05:01,420
carry the signaling circuits.

126
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,759
It is a choice made more than

127
00:05:03,759 --> 00:05:06,366
a century ago that still shapes how

128
00:05:06,366 --> 00:05:08,078
the system works today.

129
00:05:08,258 --> 00:05:10,280
When you look down at a London

130
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:12,099
track and count four rails,

131
00:05:12,099 --> 00:05:14,928
you are looking at an engineering decision

132
00:05:14,928 --> 00:05:17,152
from the early nineteen hundreds.

133
00:05:17,501 --> 00:05:19,599
Why is there a small arm beside

134
00:05:19,599 --> 00:05:21,832
the track next to subway signals?

135
00:05:22,012 --> 00:05:23,544
It is a train stop.

136
00:05:23,724 --> 00:05:25,983
When the signal is red,

137
00:05:25,983 --> 00:05:27,358
the arm rises.

138
00:05:27,538 --> 00:05:29,844
If a train tries to pass the

139
00:05:29,844 --> 00:05:32,479
red signal, the arm hits a valve

140
00:05:32,479 --> 00:05:33,796
under the train,

141
00:05:33,796 --> 00:05:35,361
called a trip cock,

142
00:05:35,361 --> 00:05:38,242
which opens the brake pipe and puts

143
00:05:38,242 --> 00:05:40,960
the train into emergency braking.

144
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:43,442
When the signal turns green,

145
00:05:43,442 --> 00:05:45,826
the arm drops down out of the

146
00:05:45,826 --> 00:05:46,155
way.

147
00:05:46,505 --> 00:05:48,540
This system is very old,

148
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:50,151
but very effective.

149
00:05:50,331 --> 00:05:52,167
It was used on the New York

150
00:05:52,167 --> 00:05:54,887
subway and the London Underground in the

151
00:05:54,887 --> 00:05:56,519
early nineteen hundreds.

152
00:05:56,699 --> 00:05:59,316
It does not depend on the driver

153
00:05:59,316 --> 00:06:00,787
seeing the signal,

154
00:06:00,787 --> 00:06:02,423
or even being awake.

155
00:06:02,603 --> 00:06:04,484
It is purely mechanical.

156
00:06:04,664 --> 00:06:07,436
If the train passes a red light,

157
00:06:07,436 --> 00:06:08,216
it stops.

158
00:06:08,566 --> 00:06:10,865
The weakness is that it only acts

159
00:06:10,865 --> 00:06:13,512
after the train has passed the signal,

160
00:06:13,512 --> 00:06:16,020
so there must be enough space beyond

161
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:18,249
the signal for the train to stop

162
00:06:18,249 --> 00:06:18,737
safely.

163
00:06:18,917 --> 00:06:23,411
Modern systems use computers and continuous communication

164
00:06:23,411 --> 00:06:26,092
to control the speed all the time.

165
00:06:26,272 --> 00:06:27,685
But in many tunnels,

166
00:06:27,685 --> 00:06:30,795
that little mechanical arm is still standing

167
00:06:30,795 --> 00:06:32,704
guard next to every signal.

168
00:06:33,053 --> 00:06:35,455
Why do some subway platforms have a

169
00:06:35,455 --> 00:06:36,827
wall of glass doors?

170
00:06:37,007 --> 00:06:39,271
They are platform screen doors.

171
00:06:39,451 --> 00:06:42,061
The doors line up exactly with the

172
00:06:42,061 --> 00:06:44,670
train doors and only open when the

173
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:45,975
train is stopped.

174
00:06:46,155 --> 00:06:48,600
Nobody can fall onto the track,

175
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,256
nobody can be pushed,

176
00:06:50,256 --> 00:06:51,833
and nobody can jump.

177
00:06:52,013 --> 00:06:54,670
They also stop litter from reaching the

178
00:06:54,670 --> 00:06:56,645
rails, which can start fires.

179
00:06:56,995 --> 00:07:00,356
One of the earliest versions opened in

180
00:07:00,356 --> 00:07:04,336
nineteen sixty-one, at Park Pobedy station in

181
00:07:04,336 --> 00:07:07,166
Leningrad, now Saint Petersburg.

182
00:07:07,346 --> 00:07:09,912
Its platform has a solid wall with

183
00:07:09,912 --> 00:07:12,930
doors, and passengers see the train only

184
00:07:12,930 --> 00:07:14,439
when the doors open.

185
00:07:14,619 --> 00:07:18,263
Today, platform doors are standard on many

186
00:07:18,263 --> 00:07:21,559
new lines, especially driverless ones,

187
00:07:21,559 --> 00:07:25,289
because no driver is watching the platform.

188
00:07:25,639 --> 00:07:28,449
Full height doors also help with air.

189
00:07:28,629 --> 00:07:31,426
They separate the station from the tunnel,

190
00:07:31,426 --> 00:07:33,890
so the station can be air conditioned

191
00:07:33,890 --> 00:07:35,755
without cooling the tunnels,

192
00:07:35,755 --> 00:07:37,953
and the wind from trains does not

193
00:07:37,953 --> 00:07:39,751
blast through the platform.

194
00:07:39,931 --> 00:07:41,724
The downside is cost,

195
00:07:41,724 --> 00:07:44,712
and the trains must stop at exactly

196
00:07:44,712 --> 00:07:45,992
the right spot,

197
00:07:45,992 --> 00:07:48,639
often within a few centimeters.

198
00:07:48,988 --> 00:07:51,615
Why are there bumpy yellow tiles near

199
00:07:51,615 --> 00:07:53,957
the edge of many train platforms?

200
00:07:54,137 --> 00:07:56,059
They are tactile paving,

201
00:07:56,059 --> 00:07:58,940
designed for people who are blind or

202
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:00,221
have low vision.

203
00:08:00,401 --> 00:08:03,466
Feet and canes can feel the bumps.

204
00:08:03,646 --> 00:08:05,716
Rows of dots mean stop,

205
00:08:05,716 --> 00:08:08,595
danger ahead, like the edge of a

206
00:08:08,595 --> 00:08:09,405
platform.

207
00:08:09,585 --> 00:08:12,158
Long raised lines mean this is the

208
00:08:12,158 --> 00:08:13,370
path, follow it.

209
00:08:13,550 --> 00:08:16,607
The bright color helps people with partial

210
00:08:16,607 --> 00:08:17,044
sight.

211
00:08:17,394 --> 00:08:20,564
Tactile paving was invented in Japan by

212
00:08:20,564 --> 00:08:23,652
Seiichi Miyake, who created it to help

213
00:08:23,652 --> 00:08:26,415
a friend who was losing his sight.

214
00:08:26,595 --> 00:08:30,247
The first tiles were installed in nineteen

215
00:08:30,247 --> 00:08:33,638
sixty-seven near a school for the blind

216
00:08:33,638 --> 00:08:34,595
in Okayama.

217
00:08:34,775 --> 00:08:37,508
Today, they are used all over the

218
00:08:37,508 --> 00:08:39,578
world, in train stations,

219
00:08:39,578 --> 00:08:42,229
at crosswalks, and at the top of

220
00:08:42,229 --> 00:08:42,809
stairs.

221
00:08:43,158 --> 00:08:46,384
The tiles also remind everyone else to

222
00:08:46,384 --> 00:08:47,233
keep back.

223
00:08:47,413 --> 00:08:50,456
That is why many platforms combine bumps

224
00:08:50,456 --> 00:08:53,346
with a painted line and announcements.

225
00:08:53,526 --> 00:08:55,947
A subway platform is one of the

226
00:08:55,947 --> 00:08:59,148
few places where millions of people stand

227
00:08:59,148 --> 00:09:01,568
a step away from a machine that

228
00:09:01,568 --> 00:09:02,661
can kill them,

229
00:09:02,661 --> 00:09:05,550
and every one of these details exists

230
00:09:05,550 --> 00:09:08,282
to keep that step from being taken.

231
00:09:08,632 --> 00:09:11,667
Why do some Paris Metro trains run

232
00:09:11,667 --> 00:09:13,096
on rubber tyres,

233
00:09:13,096 --> 00:09:14,077
like a bus?

234
00:09:14,257 --> 00:09:16,823
Rubber grips much better than steel.

235
00:09:17,003 --> 00:09:20,374
That means trains can accelerate and brake

236
00:09:20,374 --> 00:09:22,702
faster, climb steeper slopes,

237
00:09:22,702 --> 00:09:24,387
and run more quietly.

238
00:09:24,567 --> 00:09:27,090
On a metro line where stations are

239
00:09:27,090 --> 00:09:30,948
close together, faster acceleration and braking save

240
00:09:30,948 --> 00:09:32,358
time on every trip.

241
00:09:32,538 --> 00:09:36,215
Paris began using rubber tyred trains on

242
00:09:36,215 --> 00:09:39,341
line eleven in nineteen fifty-six.

243
00:09:39,690 --> 00:09:42,508
The trains run on concrete or steel

244
00:09:42,508 --> 00:09:44,360
tracks under the tyres,

245
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:48,144
and small horizontal wheels press against guide

246
00:09:48,144 --> 00:09:50,720
bars on the sides to steer them.

247
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:53,485
And hidden behind the rubber tyres are

248
00:09:53,485 --> 00:09:55,730
normal steel wheels with flanges.

249
00:09:55,910 --> 00:09:57,631
If a tyre goes flat,

250
00:09:57,631 --> 00:10:00,644
the steel wheel drops onto a normal

251
00:10:00,644 --> 00:10:03,398
rail and the train can continue.

252
00:10:03,578 --> 00:10:07,270
The French system was copied by Montreal,

253
00:10:07,270 --> 00:10:09,252
Mexico City, Santiago,

254
00:10:09,252 --> 00:10:10,242
and others.

255
00:10:10,592 --> 00:10:13,158
Rubber also has downsides.

256
00:10:13,338 --> 00:10:16,145
It wears out and must be replaced,

257
00:10:16,145 --> 00:10:18,869
it uses more energy than steel on

258
00:10:18,869 --> 00:10:21,676
steel, and it creates more heat in

259
00:10:21,676 --> 00:10:22,336
tunnels.

260
00:10:22,516 --> 00:10:25,320
That is why most new metros still

261
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:26,765
use steel wheels.

262
00:10:26,945 --> 00:10:29,472
The rubber tyre is a tool for

263
00:10:29,472 --> 00:10:33,306
specific lines, with steep hills and closely

264
00:10:33,306 --> 00:10:34,701
spaced stations.

265
00:10:35,050 --> 00:10:37,573
Why are some subway stations built higher

266
00:10:37,573 --> 00:10:39,358
than the tunnels around them?

267
00:10:39,538 --> 00:10:41,256
It saves energy.

268
00:10:41,436 --> 00:10:43,283
On several London lines,

269
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:46,592
including the Central line and the Victoria

270
00:10:46,592 --> 00:10:49,747
line, the track climbs slightly into each

271
00:10:49,747 --> 00:10:52,825
station and slopes down when leaving it.

272
00:10:53,005 --> 00:10:55,834
A train arriving uses the uphill to

273
00:10:55,834 --> 00:10:57,289
help it slow down,

274
00:10:57,289 --> 00:10:58,824
and when it leaves,

275
00:10:58,824 --> 00:11:01,330
the downhill helps it speed up.

276
00:11:01,679 --> 00:11:02,908
It sounds small,

277
00:11:02,908 --> 00:11:05,518
but a metro train stops and starts

278
00:11:05,518 --> 00:11:07,821
dozens of times on every trip.

279
00:11:08,001 --> 00:11:10,602
Gravity doing part of the braking and

280
00:11:10,602 --> 00:11:13,062
part of the acceleration adds up to

281
00:11:13,062 --> 00:11:15,733
real savings in energy and brake wear.

282
00:11:15,913 --> 00:11:19,249
The Central line opened in nineteen hundred

283
00:11:19,249 --> 00:11:20,568
with this design,

284
00:11:20,568 --> 00:11:22,275
and the Victoria line,

285
00:11:22,275 --> 00:11:25,067
opened in the late nineteen sixties,

286
00:11:25,067 --> 00:11:26,153
used it again.

287
00:11:26,503 --> 00:11:29,900
Modern trains can do something similar with

288
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:33,691
regenerative braking, sending energy back to the

289
00:11:33,691 --> 00:11:36,140
power rail when they slow down.

290
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:39,426
But humped stations need no electronics at

291
00:11:39,426 --> 00:11:39,721
all.

292
00:11:39,901 --> 00:11:42,134
It is a design that was smart

293
00:11:42,134 --> 00:11:45,059
in nineteen hundred and is still smart

294
00:11:45,059 --> 00:11:45,521
today.

295
00:11:45,701 --> 00:11:48,303
The track itself is part of the

296
00:11:48,303 --> 00:11:48,975
machine.

297
00:11:49,324 --> 00:11:51,322
Why is there a gap between the

298
00:11:51,322 --> 00:11:52,854
train and the platform?

299
00:11:53,034 --> 00:11:55,788
Because many old platforms are curved,

300
00:11:55,788 --> 00:11:57,527
and trains are straight.

301
00:11:57,707 --> 00:12:00,641
When a straight train car stops at

302
00:12:00,641 --> 00:12:02,194
a curved platform,

303
00:12:02,194 --> 00:12:04,696
the middle or the ends of the

304
00:12:04,696 --> 00:12:07,285
car are farther from the edge,

305
00:12:07,285 --> 00:12:08,493
leaving a gap.

306
00:12:08,673 --> 00:12:11,385
Platforms also cannot be too close,

307
00:12:11,385 --> 00:12:14,253
or a train swaying slightly would hit

308
00:12:14,253 --> 00:12:14,640
them.

309
00:12:14,990 --> 00:12:17,243
That is why London made mind the

310
00:12:17,243 --> 00:12:19,636
gap one of the most famous phrases

311
00:12:19,636 --> 00:12:20,552
in the world.

312
00:12:20,732 --> 00:12:24,069
The recorded announcement was introduced in the

313
00:12:24,069 --> 00:12:25,631
late nineteen sixties.

314
00:12:25,811 --> 00:12:27,538
At Embankment station,

315
00:12:27,538 --> 00:12:30,757
an old recording by actor Oswald Laurence

316
00:12:30,757 --> 00:12:32,092
played for years.

317
00:12:32,272 --> 00:12:33,328
After he died,

318
00:12:33,328 --> 00:12:35,970
his widow used to visit the station

319
00:12:35,970 --> 00:12:37,705
just to hear his voice.

320
00:12:37,885 --> 00:12:39,883
When the recording was replaced,

321
00:12:39,883 --> 00:12:42,879
the Underground brought his announcement back at

322
00:12:42,879 --> 00:12:44,190
that station for her.

323
00:12:44,539 --> 00:12:48,539
New platforms are built straight wherever possible,

324
00:12:48,539 --> 00:12:51,285
with gap fillers or platform doors.

325
00:12:51,465 --> 00:12:52,937
But in old tunnels,

326
00:12:52,937 --> 00:12:55,726
the gap is part of the architecture.

327
00:12:55,906 --> 00:12:57,481
A simple warning,

328
00:12:57,481 --> 00:13:00,538
repeated millions of times a day,

329
00:13:00,538 --> 00:13:03,873
is the cheapest safety device on the

330
00:13:03,873 --> 00:13:05,263
entire network.

331
00:13:05,613 --> 00:13:08,489
Why are subway escalators today made of

332
00:13:08,489 --> 00:13:10,922
metal, when old ones were made of

333
00:13:10,922 --> 00:13:11,291
wood?

334
00:13:11,471 --> 00:13:14,048
Because of one night in London.

335
00:13:14,228 --> 00:13:16,192
On November eighteenth,

336
00:13:16,192 --> 00:13:20,035
nineteen eighty-seven, a fire started under a

337
00:13:20,035 --> 00:13:23,110
wooden escalator at King's Cross St.

338
00:13:23,290 --> 00:13:26,924
Pancras station, probably from a dropped match.

339
00:13:27,104 --> 00:13:30,001
Grease and fluff had built up underneath

340
00:13:30,001 --> 00:13:30,726
for years.

341
00:13:30,906 --> 00:13:33,464
The fire grew slowly at first,

342
00:13:33,464 --> 00:13:37,300
then suddenly exploded up the escalator shaft

343
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:39,091
into the ticket hall.

344
00:13:39,271 --> 00:13:41,094
Thirty-one people died.

345
00:13:41,443 --> 00:13:44,759
Investigators were shocked by how fast the

346
00:13:44,759 --> 00:13:48,233
fire spread, so scientists recreated it with

347
00:13:48,233 --> 00:13:50,917
computer models and a scale model.

348
00:13:51,097 --> 00:13:53,307
They discovered something new,

349
00:13:53,307 --> 00:13:55,149
called the trench effect.

350
00:13:55,329 --> 00:13:56,410
In the sloped,

351
00:13:56,410 --> 00:13:58,649
narrow shaft of an escalator,

352
00:13:58,649 --> 00:14:01,429
the flames lie down along the steps,

353
00:14:01,429 --> 00:14:03,591
heat the wood ahead of them,

354
00:14:03,591 --> 00:14:04,826
and race upward.

355
00:14:05,006 --> 00:14:08,466
Nobody had predicted it before King's Cross.

356
00:14:08,815 --> 00:14:10,081
After the fire,

357
00:14:10,081 --> 00:14:14,045
the Underground banned smoking in all stations,

358
00:14:14,045 --> 00:14:18,262
began replacing wooden escalators with metal ones,

359
00:14:18,262 --> 00:14:21,636
installed heat detectors and sprinklers,

360
00:14:21,636 --> 00:14:24,419
and trained staff in fire safety.

361
00:14:24,599 --> 00:14:27,886
The last wooden escalator on the network

362
00:14:27,886 --> 00:14:29,859
was removed years later.

363
00:14:30,039 --> 00:14:32,136
Every metal step you ride today is

364
00:14:32,136 --> 00:14:34,172
part of the lesson of that night.

365
00:14:34,521 --> 00:14:36,623
Why do you feel a strong wind

366
00:14:36,623 --> 00:14:39,305
on the platform just before the train

367
00:14:39,305 --> 00:14:39,885
arrives?

368
00:14:40,065 --> 00:14:41,923
It is the piston effect.

369
00:14:42,103 --> 00:14:44,501
A train in a tunnel works like

370
00:14:44,501 --> 00:14:46,340
a piston in a cylinder.

371
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:48,963
It pushes a column of air ahead

372
00:14:48,963 --> 00:14:51,327
of it and pulls air behind it.

373
00:14:51,507 --> 00:14:53,557
That moving air is the wind you

374
00:14:53,557 --> 00:14:55,739
feel, and it can be strong enough

375
00:14:55,739 --> 00:14:56,929
to blow hats away.

376
00:14:57,109 --> 00:14:59,348
Vent shafts to the surface let the

377
00:14:59,348 --> 00:15:00,534
pushed air escape.

378
00:15:00,883 --> 00:15:04,517
Ventilation is also about heat and smoke.

379
00:15:04,697 --> 00:15:08,964
Trains, brakes and passengers produce enormous heat,

380
00:15:08,964 --> 00:15:11,836
and some old tunnels have warmed up

381
00:15:11,836 --> 00:15:13,231
over the decades.

382
00:15:13,411 --> 00:15:16,025
Big fans and shafts move air through

383
00:15:16,025 --> 00:15:16,824
the system.

384
00:15:17,004 --> 00:15:17,807
In a fire,

385
00:15:17,807 --> 00:15:20,296
the fans are used to push smoke

386
00:15:20,296 --> 00:15:23,427
away from people and toward the shafts,

387
00:15:23,427 --> 00:15:26,478
giving passengers clean air to escape.

388
00:15:26,827 --> 00:15:30,527
Those mysterious grates on city sidewalks,

389
00:15:30,527 --> 00:15:32,905
sometimes blowing warm air,

390
00:15:32,905 --> 00:15:34,931
are often subway vents.

391
00:15:35,111 --> 00:15:37,356
In winter, the air coming out of

392
00:15:37,356 --> 00:15:39,741
them can be warmer than the street

393
00:15:39,741 --> 00:15:40,161
above.

394
00:15:40,341 --> 00:15:42,506
Next time you stand over one,

395
00:15:42,506 --> 00:15:45,492
you are standing on the breathing system

396
00:15:45,492 --> 00:15:47,807
of a giant underground machine.

397
00:15:48,156 --> 00:15:50,478
So here are the eleven.

398
00:15:50,658 --> 00:15:53,015
A pit that gives people a place

399
00:15:53,015 --> 00:15:53,851
to survive.

400
00:15:54,031 --> 00:15:55,533
A rail with a roof,

401
00:15:55,533 --> 00:15:58,379
and a fourth rail that protects iron

402
00:15:58,379 --> 00:15:59,012
tunnels.

403
00:15:59,192 --> 00:16:01,692
An arm that stops trains at red

404
00:16:01,692 --> 00:16:02,257
lights.

405
00:16:02,437 --> 00:16:05,454
Glass walls, and bumpy tiles for those

406
00:16:05,454 --> 00:16:07,359
who cannot see the edge.

407
00:16:07,539 --> 00:16:10,786
Rubber tyres, stations built on hills,

408
00:16:10,786 --> 00:16:12,495
a gap you must mind,

409
00:16:12,495 --> 00:16:14,631
escalators made of metal,

410
00:16:14,631 --> 00:16:17,109
and a wind that tells you the

411
00:16:17,109 --> 00:16:18,476
train is coming.

412
00:16:18,826 --> 00:16:21,406
A subway station looks like a simple

413
00:16:21,406 --> 00:16:23,842
room with a train passing through.

414
00:16:24,022 --> 00:16:26,917
It is actually a carefully designed machine

415
00:16:26,917 --> 00:16:29,409
for keeping millions of people alive.

416
00:16:29,589 --> 00:16:32,685
If you want more machines explained part

417
00:16:32,685 --> 00:16:35,626
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

418
00:16:35,806 --> 00:16:37,781
And tell us in the comments which

419
00:16:37,781 --> 00:16:39,756
machine you want us to take apart

420
00:16:39,756 --> 00:16:40,055
next.
